'use strict';

window.TUTORIALS_DATA_ADVANCED = {
    id: 'advanced',
    title: 'Advanced Systems',
    topics: [
        {
            id: 'image-generator-overview',
            title: 'Image Generator Overview',
            content: "# Image Generator\n\nHOWL includes a built-in **Stable Diffusion** image generator for creating AI art. It runs locally on your hardware using .safetensors and .ckpt model files.\n\n## What You Need\n\n1. **SD Models** — .safetensors or .ckpt checkpoint files.\n2. **SD Server** — The stable-diffusion-cpp or equivalent executable.\n3. **VRAM** — At least 4 GB for basic generation, 8+ GB for high resolution.\n\n## Accessing the Image Generator\n\nNavigate to **Image Generator** from the main navigation or use the sidebar shortcut.\n\n## Generation Modes\n\n| Mode | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **txt2img** | Generate from text prompt only. |\n| **img2img** | Generate from text + an existing image. |\n| **Inpaint** | Edit specific regions of an image using a mask. |\n| **Outpaint** | Extend an image beyond its original borders. |\n| **Upscale** | Increase image resolution. |\n\n## Interface Layout\n\n- **Left panel**: Generation controls (prompt, settings, model selection).\n- **Center**: Preview area for generated images.\n- **Right**: History panel showing past generations.\n- **Bottom**: Diagnostics panel (memory, goroutines, engine status).\n\n<info>\nThe Image Generator is completely separate from the chat LLM. You can generate images even when no chat model is loaded.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'image-generator-models',
            title: 'Selecting & Loading Models',
            content: "# Selecting & Loading Models\n\n## Engine Paths\n\nBefore generating images, set two paths:\n\n1. **Models Directory** — Where your .safetensors and .ckpt files are stored.\n2. **SD Server Path** — Path to the stable-diffusion-cpp executable.\n\nClick **Browse** next to each field or enter paths manually. Click **Apply Paths** to save.\n\n## Model Discovery\n\nOnce paths are set, HOWL scans the models directory and lists all compatible files.\n\n| Format | Compatibility |\n|--------|--------------|\n| **.safetensors** | Fully supported. Recommended. |\n| **.ckpt** | Supported. Legacy format. |\n| **.gguf** | Limited support. Check compatibility. |\n\n## Loading a Model\n\n1. Select a model from the dropdown.\n2. Click **Load Model**.\n3. The model loads into VRAM/RAM.\n4. Status shows \"Loaded: [model name]\".\n\n## Model Presets\n\nWhen you load a model, HOWL auto-applies presets based on the model family:\n\n| Family | Steps | CFG | Notes |\n|--------|-------|-----|-------|\n| **Flux** | 4 | 1.0 | Fast, high quality. Use Euler sampler. |\n| **SDXL** | 20-30 | 7-8 | High resolution. Use DPM++ 2M Karras. |\n| **SD 1.5** | 20-25 | 7 | Standard. Use Euler a or DPM++ 2M. |\n\n<warning>\nModels incompatible with your SD server version will fail to load. Update your SD server if you encounter \"unknown format\" errors.\n</warning>"
        },
        {
            id: 'image-generator-generation',
            title: 'Generating Images',
            content: "# Generating Images\n\n## txt2img (Text-to-Image)\n\n1. Enter a **Positive Prompt** describing what you want.\n2. (Optional) Enter a **Negative Prompt** describing what to avoid.\n3. Adjust **CFG Scale**, **Steps**, **Sampler**, **Size**.\n4. Set a **Seed** (-1 for random, or a fixed number for reproducibility).\n5. Click **Generate**.\n\n## Prompt Engineering Tips\n\n- Be specific: \"a red dragon breathing fire in a dark cave\" beats \"dragon.\"\n- Use quality tags: \"masterpiece, best quality, highly detailed, 8k uhd.\"\n- Use negative prompts: \"blurry, low quality, deformed, extra limbs.\"\n- Specify style: \"oil painting, digital art, photograph, anime.\"\n- Specify lighting: \"dramatic lighting, golden hour, soft shadows.\"\n\n## img2img (Image-to-Image)\n\n1. Generate or load an image into the preview area.\n2. Set the **Denoising Strength** (0.0 = no change, 1.0 = complete redraw).\n3. Enter your prompt.\n4. Click **Generate**.\n\nCommon denoising strengths:\n- **0.3-0.4** — Minor variations, style transfer.\n- **0.5-0.6** — Significant changes while keeping composition.\n- **0.7-1.0** — Heavy redraw, nearly new image.\n\n## Inpaint\n\n1. Load an image.\n2. Draw a mask on the image (the masked area will be regenerated).\n3. Enter a prompt describing what should replace the masked region.\n4. Click **Generate**.\n\n<info>\nGeneration speed depends on Steps, image size, and your hardware. A 512x512 image at 20 steps takes 5-15 seconds on a mid-range GPU.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'image-generator-settings',
            title: 'Image Generator Settings',
            content: "# Image Generator Settings\n\n## CFG Scale (Classifier-Free Guidance)\n\n**Range**: 1.0 to 30.0\n**Default**: 7.0\n\nControls how strictly the model follows your prompt.\n\n- **1.0-3.0** → Very loose. Creative but may ignore the prompt.\n- **5.0-7.0** → Balanced. Good default.\n- **10.0-15.0** → Strict. Follows prompt closely but may look artificial.\n- **20.0+** → Extreme. Often over-saturated and repetitive.\n\n## Steps (Sampling Steps)\n\n**Range**: 1 to 150\n**Default**: 20\n\nNumber of denoising iterations.\n\n- **4** (Flux) → Minimum for quality.\n- **20-25** → Standard. Good quality.\n- **30-50** → Higher quality. Diminishing returns.\n- **50+** → Very slow. Rarely worth it.\n\n## Sampler\n\nThe algorithm used for denoising:\n\n| Sampler | Speed | Quality | Best For |\n|---------|-------|---------|----------|\n| **Euler** | Fast | Good | General use |\n| **Euler a** | Fast | Good | Variety |\n| **DPM++ 2M** | Medium | Very Good | Quality |\n| **DPM++ 2M Karras** | Medium | Excellent | SDXL, high quality |\n| **DDIM** | Medium | Good | Deterministic |\n\n## Seed\n\n- **-1** → Random seed each time.\n- **Fixed number** → Reproducible results.\n\n## Image Size\n\nStandard sizes:\n- **512x512** — Fast, low VRAM.\n- **768x768** — Balanced.\n- **1024x1024** — High quality. Needs more VRAM.\n- **1216x832** (SDXL portrait) — Vertical.\n- **1344x768** (SDXL landscape) — Widescreen.\n\n<warning>\nLarger images need exponentially more VRAM. A 1024x1024 image uses ~4x the memory of 512x512.\n</warning>"
        },
        {
            id: 'image-generator-history',
            title: 'History & Save Location',
            content: "# History & Save Location\n\n## History Panel\n\nThe right panel shows all previously generated images.\n\n- Click an image to view it in the preview area.\n- Click the **trash icon** to delete an image.\n- Images persist across app restarts.\n\n## Default Save Location\n\nGenerated images are saved to:\n\n```\n[App Data Directory]/images/\n```\n\nThe exact path depends on your OS:\n- **Windows**: `%APPDATA%/HOWL-Chat/images/`\n- **Linux**: `~/.local/share/HOWL-Chat/images/`\n- **macOS**: `~/Library/Application Support/HOWL-Chat/images/`\n\n## Metadata\n\nEach saved image includes metadata:\n- Positive prompt\n- Negative prompt\n- CFG, Steps, Sampler, Seed\n- Model name\n- Generation timestamp\n\nThis metadata is embedded in the image file (PNG chunks) and can be read by compatible tools.\n\n## Reusing Images\n\n1. Click a history image to load it into the preview area.\n2. Use it as an **init image** for img2img.\n3. Or use it as a **mask base** for inpainting.\n\n<info>\nImages are stored locally. Nothing is uploaded to the cloud unless you explicitly use a remote endpoint.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'audio-overview',
            title: 'Audio Settings Overview',
            content: "# Audio Settings Overview\n\nAudio Settings control speech recognition (ASR), text-to-speech (TTS), voice activity detection (VAD), and audio hardware configuration. Access via **Settings → Audio Settings**.\n\n## Five Tabs\n\n- **General**: Enable/disable ASR and TTS, TTS autoplay, push-to-talk, TTS volume\n- **Speech Recognition (ASR)**: ASR engine, language, device, threads, beam size, temperature, translation\n- **Text-to-Speech (TTS)**: TTS engine, voice model, speed, noise scale, sentence silence, caching\n- **VAD**: Voice activity detection, speech threshold, min speech/silence duration, padding\n- **Hardware**: Input/output devices, sample rate, channels, bit depth, microphone sensitivity\n\n<info>\nAll audio settings persist automatically across sessions. Changes apply immediately.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'audio-general',
            title: 'Audio: General Settings',
            content: "# Audio: General Settings\n\n## Enable Speech Recognition (ASR)\n\n**Toggle** | **Default**: false\n\nEnables voice input via microphone. Requires whisper-cli or compatible ASR engine.\n\n- **Disabled**: Microphone button is hidden. Voice input unavailable.\n- **Enabled**: Microphone button appears in chat input bar. Can speak messages.\n\n## Enable Text-to-Speech (TTS)\n\n**Toggle** | **Default**: false\n\nEnables AI responses to be spoken aloud. Requires Piper, Kokoro, or MeloTTS backend.\n\n- **Disabled**: No audio output from AI responses.\n- **Enabled**: AI responses can be spoken via TTS engine.\n\n## Auto-play TTS on AI Messages\n\n**Toggle** | **Default**: true\n\nAutomatically speaks every AI response when it completes.\n\n- **Disabled**: Click speaker icon on messages to hear them.\n- **Enabled**: Responses play automatically as soon as generation finishes.\n\n## Push-to-Talk Mode\n\n**Toggle** | **Default**: true\n\nControls microphone button behavior.\n\n- **Enabled (Push-to-Talk)**: Hold mic button while speaking. Release to send.\n- **Disabled (Toggle)**: Click once to start recording, click again to stop and send.\n\n## TTS Output Volume\n\n**Slider**: 0 to 1 | **Step**: 0.05 | **Default**: 0.8\n\nMaster volume for all text-to-speech playback.\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **0** | Muted. No TTS audio. |\n| **0.5** | Half volume. |\n| **0.8** | Default. Comfortable listening level. |\n| **1.0** | Maximum volume. |\n\n<info>\nTTS volume affects all TTS output regardless of voice or engine. Use system volume for overall control.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'audio-asr',
            title: 'Audio: Speech Recognition (ASR)',
            content: "# Audio: Speech Recognition (ASR)\n\n## ASR Engine\n\n**Select**: Auto, Whisper Tiny/Base/Small/Medium/Large, Qwen ASR | **Default**: auto\n\nSelect the speech recognition backend.\n\n| Value | VRAM/RAM | Speed | Accuracy | Best For |\n|-------|----------|-------|----------|----------|\n| **Auto** | Varies | Varies | Varies | Recommended. Picks based on hardware. |\n| **Whisper Tiny** | ~1 GB | Fastest | Lower | Low-end systems, quick drafts. |\n| **Whisper Base** | ~1.4 GB | Fast | Good | Balanced. |\n| **Whisper Small** | ~2 GB | Medium | Better | Most users. |\n| **Whisper Medium** | ~5 GB | Slower | Very Good | High accuracy needed. |\n| **Whisper Large** | ~10 GB | Slowest | Best | Maximum accuracy. |\n| **Qwen ASR** | Varies | Varies | Good | API-based, multilingual. |\n\n<warning>\nLarge models (Medium/Large) require 8+ GB VRAM or 16+ GB RAM. Use Tiny/Base on low-end systems.\n</warning>\n\n## Spoken Language\n\n**Select**: Auto-Detect, English, Spanish, French, German, Italian, Portuguese, Japanese, Chinese, Korean, Russian, Dutch, Polish | **Default**: auto\n\nLanguage for transcription.\n\n- **Auto-Detect**: Attempts to detect from audio. Slower but flexible.\n- **Specific language**: Faster and more accurate if you know the language.\n\n## Compute Device\n\n**Select**: Auto-Select, CPU Only, CUDA (NVIDIA GPU) | **Default**: auto\n\nHardware to run ASR inference on.\n\n- **Auto-Select**: Uses GPU if available, falls back to CPU.\n- **CPU Only**: Forces CPU inference. Slower but works on any system.\n- **CUDA**: Forces NVIDIA GPU. Fastest if you have compatible GPU.\n\n## CPU Threads\n\n**Slider**: 1 to 16 | **Step**: 1 | **Default**: 4\n\nParallel computation threads for CPU inference.\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **1-4** | Low CPU usage. Slower. |\n| **4-8** | Balanced. |\n| **8-16** | Higher CPU usage. Faster. |\n\n## Beam Size\n\n**Slider**: 1 to 10 | **Step**: 1 | **Default**: 5\n\nNumber of beams for beam-search decoding. Higher = more accurate but slower.\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **1** | Greedy decoding. Fastest. |\n| **5** | Default. Good balance. |\n| **10** | Maximum accuracy. Slower. |\n\n## Best-of (Candidates)\n\n**Slider**: 1 to 10 | **Step**: 1 | **Default**: 5\n\nNumber of candidate transcriptions to evaluate. Higher = better accuracy.\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **1** | Single candidate. Fastest. |\n| **5** | Default. Good balance. |\n| **10** | Maximum accuracy. Slower. |\n\n## Temperature\n\n**Slider**: 0 to 1 | **Step**: 0.05 | **Default**: 0.0\n\nSampling randomness. Lower is more accurate.\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **0.0** | Deterministic. Most accurate. |\n| **0.5** | Some randomness. |\n| **1.0** | Maximum randomness. Less accurate. |\n\n## Translate to English\n\n**Toggle** | **Default**: false\n\nAutomatically translate all recognized speech to English before sending.\n\n- **Disabled**: Transcribes in original language.\n- **Enabled**: Transcribes then translates to English. Useful for non-English users.\n\n## Suppress Timestamps\n\n**Toggle** | **Default**: false\n\nRemove timestamps from transcription output.\n\n- **Disabled**: Includes timestamps (e.g., \"[00:00:00] Hello\").\n- **Enabled**: Cleaner text output. Loses timing info.\n\n## Flash Attention\n\n**Toggle** | **Default**: true\n\nEnable Flash Attention for faster inference on modern GPUs.\n\n- **Enabled**: 2-3x faster on modern GPUs (Ampere+). Recommended.\n- **Disabled**: Disable if encountering errors on older GPUs.\n\n<warning>\nFlash Attention requires CUDA 11.8+ and modern GPU architecture. Disable if you get errors.\n</warning>"
        },
        {
            id: 'audio-tts',
            title: 'Audio: Text-to-Speech (TTS)',
            content: "# Audio: Text-to-Speech (TTS)\n\n## TTS Engine\n\n**Select**: Piper, Kokoro, MeloTTS, Browser TTS, Auto | **Default**: piper\n\nSelect the speech synthesis backend.\n\n| Value | Type | Quality | Requirements |\n|-------|------|---------|---------------|\n| **Piper** | Local | Good, fast | Python + pip install piper-tts |\n| **Kokoro** | Local | High quality (neural ONNX) | Python + kokoro package |\n| **MeloTTS** | Remote API | Excellent | HOWL_MELOTTS_URL environment variable |\n| **Browser TTS** | Local fallback | Basic | No install required |\n| **Auto** | Auto-select | Varies | Picks best available |\n\n<warning>\nMeloTTS requires HOWL_MELOTTS_URL environment variable. Kokoro requires Python + kokoro package.\n</warning>\n\n## TTS Voice Model\n\n**Select**: en_US-lessac-medium, en_US-amy-medium, en_GB-southern_male-medium | **Default**: en_US-lessac-medium\n\nVoice model for Piper. Install additional .onnx models to the models/ folder.\n\n| Value | Gender | Accent |\n|-------|--------|--------|\n| **en_US-lessac-medium** | Male | American |\n| **en_US-amy-medium** | Female | American |\n| **en_GB-southern_male-medium** | Male | British Southern |\n\n## Speech Speed\n\n**Slider**: 0.5 to 2.0 | **Step**: 0.05 | **Default**: 1.0\n\nPlayback speed multiplier.\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **0.5** | Half speed. Slow, deliberate. |\n| **1.0** | Normal speed. Default. |\n| **1.5** | 1.5x speed. |\n| **2.0** | Double speed. Fast. |\n\n## Noise Scale\n\n**Slider**: 0 to 1 | **Step**: 0.01 | **Default**: 0.667\n\nPiper-specific: randomness in audio generation. Lower = cleaner, more robotic.\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **0** | Deterministic. Very robotic. |\n| **0.667** | Default. Balanced. |\n| **1.0** | Maximum randomness. More natural but less consistent. |\n\n## Noise Width\n\n**Slider**: 0 to 1 | **Step**: 0.01 | **Default**: 0.8\n\nPiper-specific: phoneme width noise. Affects naturalness of speech.\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **0** | Narrow. Crisp but artificial. |\n| **0.8** | Default. Balanced. |\n| **1.0** | Wide. More natural but potentially blurry. |\n\n## Sentence Silence\n\n**Slider**: 0 to 2 | **Step**: 0.05 | **Default**: 0.2\n\nSeconds of silence added after each sentence.\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **0** | No pause. Sentences run together. |\n| **0.2** | Default. Natural pause. |\n| **1.0** | Long pause. Dramatic effect. |\n\n## Enable TTS Cache\n\n**Toggle** | **Default**: true\n\nCache synthesized audio to disk to avoid re-generating identical phrases.\n\n- **Enabled**: Faster for repeated phrases. Uses disk space.\n- **Disabled**: Always regenerates. Slower but saves disk space.\n\n## Cache Size Limit\n\n**Slider**: 50 to 1024 | **Step**: 50 | **Default**: 256 | **Unit**: MB\n\nMaximum disk space for TTS cache. Oldest files are removed first.\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **50 MB** | Small cache. Frequent evictions. |\n| **256 MB** | Default. Good for most users. |\n| **1024 MB** | Large cache. Stores many phrases. |\n\n<info>\nTTS cache is stored in the app data directory. Delete the cache folder to clear it manually.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'audio-vad',
            title: 'Audio: Voice Activity Detection (VAD)',
            content: "# Audio: Voice Activity Detection (VAD)\n\nVAD automatically splits speech and removes silence. Recommended for ASR.\n\n## Enable VAD\n\n**Toggle** | **Default**: false\n\nEnable Voice Activity Detection.\n\n- **Disabled**: ASR processes entire audio including silence. Slower, less accurate.\n- **Enabled**: VAD splits audio into speech segments, removes silence. Faster, more accurate.\n\n<warning>\nVAD requires a compatible model. May increase latency slightly.\n</warning>\n\n## Speech Threshold\n\n**Slider**: 0 to 1 | **Step**: 0.05 | **Default**: 0.5\n\nConfidence level required to classify audio as speech. Higher = fewer false positives.\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **0** | Everything is speech. Many false positives. |\n| **0.5** | Default. Balanced. |\n| **0.8** | Strict. Only clear speech detected. |\n| **1.0** | Very strict. May miss quiet speech. |\n\n## Min Speech Duration\n\n**Slider**: 0 to 1000 | **Step**: 50 | **Default**: 250 | **Unit**: ms\n\nMinimum milliseconds of speech required before a segment is kept. Filters out short noises.\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **0 ms** | Keep all speech. |\n| **250 ms** | Default. Filters clicks, pops. |\n| **500 ms** | Filters short utterances. |\n\n## Min Silence Duration\n\n**Slider**: 0 to 1000 | **Step**: 50 | **Default**: 100 | **Unit**: ms\n\nMilliseconds of silence required to split speech into separate segments.\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **0 ms** | Split on any silence. Many segments. |\n| **100 ms** | Default. Natural sentence breaks. |\n| **500 ms** | Longer pauses required. Fewer segments. |\n\n## Speech Padding\n\n**Slider**: 0 to 500 | **Step**: 10 | **Default**: 30 | **Unit**: ms\n\nMilliseconds of padding added before and after detected speech segments.\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **0 ms** | No padding. May cut off start/end of words. |\n| **30 ms** | Default. Preserves word boundaries. |\n| **100 ms** | Generous padding. Slower but safer. |\n\n<info>\nVAD is most useful for push-to-talk mode. For toggle mode, it helps distinguish speech from background noise.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'audio-hardware',
            title: 'Audio: Hardware & I/O',
            content: "# Audio: Hardware & I/O\n\n## Input Device\n\n**Select**: System Default, enumerated devices, No Audio Input | **Default**: default\n\nMicrophone or audio input source for speech recognition.\n\n- **System Default**: Uses OS default microphone.\n- **Specific device**: Select from enumerated list.\n- **No Audio Input**: Disable microphone input.\n\nClick **Refresh** to re-enumerate devices after connecting/disconnecting microphones.\n\n## Output Device\n\n**Select**: System Default, enumerated devices, No Audio Output | **Default**: default\n\nSpeaker or audio output device for TTS playback.\n\n- **System Default**: Uses OS default speakers.\n- **Specific device**: Select from enumerated list.\n- **No Audio Output**: Disable audio output.\n\nClick **Refresh** to re-enumerate devices after connecting/disconnecting speakers.\n\n## Sample Rate\n\n**Select**: 16000 Hz (ASR Optimal), 22050 Hz (Piper Default), 44100 Hz (CD Quality), 48000 Hz (Studio) | **Default**: 16000\n\nAudio sample rate for recording and playback.\n\n| Value | Use Case |\n|-------|----------|\n| **16000 Hz** | ASR optimal. Best for speech recognition. |\n| **22050 Hz** | Piper default. Good for TTS. |\n| **44100 Hz** | CD quality. General use. |\n| **48000 Hz** | Studio quality. Professional use. |\n\n<info>\n16 kHz is optimal for ASR. Higher sample rates don't improve ASR accuracy but use more CPU.\n</info>\n\n## Audio Channels\n\n**Select**: Mono (1 channel), Stereo (2 channels) | **Default**: mono\n\nMono is required for ASR. Stereo is supported for playback.\n\n- **Mono**: 1 channel. Required for ASR. Works for TTS.\n- **Stereo**: 2 channels. Not supported for ASR. Better for music playback.\n\n## Bit Depth\n\n**Select**: 16-bit (Standard), 24-bit (High Dynamic Range), 32-bit float (Professional) | **Default**: 16\n\nAudio bit depth for recording.\n\n| Value | Dynamic Range | Use Case |\n|-------|---------------|----------|\n| **16-bit** | 96 dB | Standard. CD quality. |\n| **24-bit** | 144 dB | High dynamic range. Professional recording. |\n| **32-bit float** | Virtually unlimited | Studio production. |\n\n## Microphone Sensitivity\n\n**Slider**: 0 to 1 | **Step**: 0.05 | **Default**: 0.5\n\nInput gain for microphone recording.\n\n| Value | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **0** | No input. |\n| **0.3** | Low gain. For loud environments. |\n| **0.5** | Default. Balanced. |\n| **0.8** | High gain. For quiet speech. |\n| **1.0** | Maximum gain. May cause clipping. |\n\n<warning>\nIf speech is too quiet, increase sensitivity. If audio is distorted or clipping, decrease sensitivity.\n</warning>"
        },
        {
            id: 'vision-overview',
            title: 'Vision Support',
            content: "# Vision Support\n\nVision-capable models can analyze images you attach to chat messages.\n\n## How It Works\n\n1. Attach an image to your message (see Chat → Image Attachments).\n2. The image is resized to 1024px max and encoded as JPEG.\n3. The model receives both your text and the image.\n4. The model describes, analyzes, or responds based on what it \"sees.\"\n\n## What the Model Sees\n\nThe model does not see previous images unless they are still attached. Each message is independent.\n\n## Supported Model Families (2026)\n\n| Model | VRAM (FP16) | Best For |\n|-------|-------------|----------|\n| **LLaVA 1.6 / LLaVA-NeXT** | 12-16 GB | General vision tasks |\n| **Qwen2-VL** | 10-14 GB | Multilingual, high-res |\n| **InternVL2** | 12-16 GB | Document understanding |\n| **MiniCPM-V 2.6** | 6-8 GB | Low VRAM, good quality |\n| **Llama 3.2 Vision** | 10-14 GB | Strong reasoning + vision |\n\n## Common Use Cases\n\n- **Describe an image**: \"What do you see in this picture?\"\n- **Read text**: \"What does this sign say?\"\n- **Analyze art**: \"Describe the composition and mood.\"\n- **Identify objects**: \"What kind of bird is this?\"\n- **Code review**: \"Find the bug in this screenshot.\"\n\n<warning>\nNot all GGUF models support vision. Models must be specifically trained with vision encoder layers. Look for \"-vision\" or \"-vl\" in the model name.\n</warning>"
        },
        {
            id: 'rag-overview',
            title: 'RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)',
            content: "# RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)\n\nRAG lets you upload documents (PDFs, text files) and have the AI reference them during conversations.\n\n## What RAG Does\n\n1. You upload a document.\n2. The document is split into chunks and stored in a vector database.\n3. When you send a message, HOWL searches the document for relevant passages.\n4. The most relevant passages are injected into the context window.\n5. The model generates a response informed by the document.\n\n## Uploading Documents\n\n1. Go to **Story Tools**.\n2. Open the **Knowledge** panel.\n3. Click **Upload Document**.\n4. Select a file (PDF, TXT, MD supported).\n5. Choose the scope: **World**, **Scenario**, or **Character**.\n\n## Scope\n\n| Scope | When It Applies |\n|-------|-----------------|\n| **World** | Any chat using this world. |\n| **Scenario** | Any chat using this scenario. |\n| **Character** | Only when this character is present. |\n\n## Managing Documents\n\n- View all uploaded documents in the Knowledge panel.\n- Delete documents to remove them from the vector store.\n- Refresh the knowledge base if you update a document.\n\n<info>\nRAG is local. Documents are processed on your machine and stored locally. No data leaves your device.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'memories-overview',
            title: 'Memories',
            content: "# Memories\n\nMemories are persistent facts the AI remembers across messages, sessions, and even app restarts.\n\n## Two Types of Memories\n\n| Type | How Created | Scope |\n|------|-------------|-------|\n| **Automated** | Generated by the system from significant chat events. | Character, World, or Global. |\n| **Manual** | Created by you in Story Tools. | Any scope. |\n\n## Automated Memories\n\nThe system watches for significant events:\n- Character revelations\n- User preferences stated in chat\n- World state changes\n- Important plot developments\n\nWhen detected, a memory entry is created and stored.\n\n## Manual Memories\n\n1. Go to **Story Tools**.\n2. Open the **Memories** panel.\n3. Click **Add Memory**.\n4. Write the memory content.\n5. Set scope, confidence, and pin status.\n6. Save.\n\n## Memory Properties\n\n- **Content** — The fact being remembered.\n- **Scope** — Character, World, or Global.\n- **Confidence** — How certain the system is (0-100%).\n- **Pinned** — Pinned memories never decay and are always included.\n- **Decay** — Unpinned memories may fade over time unless reinforced.\n\n## Memory Integration\n\nToggle **Memory Integration** in Model Settings:\n- **Enabled** → Memories are injected into every generation context.\n- **Disabled** → The AI has no persistent memory beyond the context window.\n\n## Viewing Memories\n\n- **Memory Flyout** — Click the memory icon in the sidebar to see recent memories.\n- **Character Memory Viewer** — Right-click a character in the sidebar → View Memories.\n- **Story Tools** — Full memory management interface.\n\n<info>\nMemories are stored in the backend and persist across sessions. They are one of HOWL's most powerful features — the more you chat, the more the AI \"learns\" about your preferences and the story.\n</info>"
        }
    ]
};
